 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Between Vbb (pin 3 and the tab) and OUT sits an N-channel power MOSFET with a body diode. Its gate is driven by a charge pump, level shifter and rectifier, governed by logic fed from IN through an ESD stage. Blocks for overvoltage protection, current limiting, gate protection, a clamp for unclamped inductive loads and a temperature sensor act on the gate. A voltage source makes the internal logic supply. The load returns to load ground; the logic returns through GND to signal ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "IN is referred to GND. The output turns on at 2.2 V or above and off at 0.8 V or below, with about 0.3 V of hysteresis; the thresholds sit near 1.7 V and 1.35 V at 25 °C and fall slightly with temperature and, for turn-off, with Vbb. The input current is 3 to 25 µA at 5 V. The pin may see -10 V to +16 V continuously and ±5 mA DC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "With a resistive load, ton is measured from the input edge to 90% of VOUT and toff to 10%; the turn-on slew rate over 10 to 30% and the turn-off slew rate over 70 to 40% of VOUT. Both are about 0.8 V/µs at 13.5 V and 47 Ω, and both slew rates rise with Vbb. A lamp draws an inrush peak at turn-on that settles to its running current. If Vbb arrives while IN is high, the output comes on with the supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inductive loads",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "At turn-off of an inductive load, OUT is driven below ground until the drain-source voltage reaches the clamp VON(CL), 47 V typ and 41 V min, and holds there while the load current decays. The switch absorbs EAS: 4.4 J is the limit for one pulse at Tj = 150 °C, Vbb = 13.5 V, IL = 1 A, and the allowed inductance and energy fall steeply as the load current rises."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Short circuit and current limit",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Turned on into a short, the current first peaks at IL(SCp), 14 A typ at 25 °C, falling with temperature (at most 20 A at -40 °C, at least 7 A at 150 °C), and then sags as the chip heats. After toff(SC), around 1.6 ms from 25 °C at 20 V, the thermal sensor turns the output off; once the chip cools it restarts, and while IN stays high it cycles with a repetitive limit IL(SCr) of 10 A typ. The chip can take several milliseconds to heat up, set by the external conditions. Full short-circuit protection holds up to Vbb(SC) = 32 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "The output turns off when the junction reaches Tjt, 150 °C min, and comes back on by itself once the junction has cooled by the hysteresis of about 10 K, as long as IN is still high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Undervoltage",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Below Vbb(under) the charge pump stops and the output turns off (4 V max up to 85 °C, 5.5 V max at 150 °C). Rising again, it restarts at Vbb(u cp), 4 V typ and 5.5 V max, so the drain-source voltage follows Vbb while off and drops low once the pump restarts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overvoltage and load dump",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A zener VZ2 of 47 V typ (Vbb(AZ), 41 V min) from Vbb to GND protects the logic, and a 6.1 V zener VZ1 guards the input behind RI. Above Vbb(AZ) the current into GND must be limited from outside, typically with RGND = 150 Ω. The part withstands a 75 V load dump (VA = 13.5 V, RI = 2 Ω, td = 400 ms, RL = 13.5 Ω) with the input high or low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse battery",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "With 150 Ω in the GND lead the part survives -32 V on Vbb. The load current then runs backwards through the body diode (about 600 mV) and only the load limits it; dissipation is higher than in normal operation and there is no temperature protection. The input current must stay within ±5 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Loss of ground and loss of supply",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The part is protected against a lost GND connection and a lost Vbb connection. The cases covered are GND opened, GND opened while pulled up by a resistor divider from Vbb, and Vbb opened with IN high while an inductive load carries current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Junction to case is 3 K/W max. On an FR4 board with 6 cm² of 70 µm copper on the drain, junction to ambient is 45 K/W typ (60 K/W max); on the minimum footprint it is 80 K/W typ. Ptot is 41.6 W, and IL(ISO) of 3.5 A min is rated at TC = 85 °C with 0.5 V across the switch. RON roughly doubles from 25 °C to 150 °C and is flat above about 9 V of supply."
  }
 ],
